  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nTHIS AFTERNOON: A weak frontal boundary has moved through the \nforecast area this afternoon. A warm westerly northwesterly flow \nbehind the front continues to allow temperatures to surge into the \n60s. By this evening, a light to even variable wind may develop.\n\nTONIGHT: Outside of scattered high lvl cirrus, a rather quiet \novernight period is expected. The light and variable winds that \ndevelop will promote strong radiational cooling, dropping low \ntemperatures near the freezing mark.  Some high resolution models \nsuch as the HRRR/RRFS/RAP indicate enough cooling for valley/river \nbased fog to develop. \n\nTUESDAY: Southwesterly surface winds will increase through the day \non Tuesday as a progressive open wave begins to cross the Northern \nRockies. This wave induces surface cyclogenesis across the \nwestern Dakota early in the day, increasing the SPG over the \nTri-State area. With modest low-lvl warm advection in place, \nit's possible that temperature may warm higher than what the NBM\nsuggests. Will trend temperatures towards the 90th percentile, \nindicating upper 50s to lower 60s. The relatively moisture \nstarved trough will move through the Plains Tuesday night, \npushing the surface low into Western Minnesota late in the \nevening and bringing strong cold advection through the Dakotas. \nWind gusts through the James and Missouri River valleys have \npotential to reach the 35 to 45 mph range which has led me to \nbump winds upwards. Some questions continue on the depth of the \nlow-lvl moisture field, which could produce a sprinkle as it \nmoves through. The latest ensemble guidance continues to back \noff on the probabilities of any measurable rain (<20%). \n\nWEDNESDAY:  By Wednesday morning, the development of a large ridge \nover the western CONUS and a trough over the eastern CONUS will put \nthe CWA into zone of strong cold advection. The initial surge of \ncold air early Wednesday won't be nearly as strong as the secondary \nsurge that arrives Wednesday night.  Thus, temperatures Wednesday \nafternoon may still rise into the 50s.\n\nTHURSDAY-FRIDAY: Under the influence of a modifying arctic airmass \nat the end of the week, temperatures will fall below normal with \nhighs only near 40 degrees and morning lows likely to fall into the \nmiddle 20s by Friday morning. No precipitation is expected. \n\nSATURDAY-MONDAY:  Regional weather will remain very quiet \nthrough next weekend and into the early portions of next week.  Mid-\nlvl ridging will slowly transition eastward during the weekend, \nallowing a slow increase in southerly to southeasterly surface winds \nSaturday. These winds will increase further on Sunday, introducing \nvery low fire danger risks with gusts over 20-30 mph.  Temperatures \nwill continue to warm through the weekend, but slowly as low-lvl \nwarm advection is not intense, and SE surface flow is less conducive \nto strong warming. Nevertheless, by Sunday and Monday we'll see \nhighs back above normal into the 50s.",
